      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Sealant does not fail because the tube was cheap. It fails because it was applied onto a surface that still had the last bead's residue on it. Removal is not the boring part before the real job — it is most of the real job.
        </p>

        <h2 id="why-it-matters" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. Why Removal Decides Everything
        </h2>

        <p>
          A sealant bead works by bonding to two faces and stretching between them. That bond is a chemical and mechanical grip on the substrate itself — the porcelain, the tile glaze, the painted wood, the vinyl frame. Anything sitting between the sealant and that substrate becomes the weakest link in the chain, and the joint will fail at that layer rather than in the sealant.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is why caulking over an existing bead so often disappoints. The new material may be excellent. It may be rated for fifty per cent movement and twenty-five years of UV. None of that matters if it is bonded to a strip of ten-year-old acrylic that is itself only loosely attached to the tub. When the joint moves, the old bead lets go, and the new one comes away with it looking perfectly intact — which is the confusing part, because the failure looks like an adhesion problem with the new product.
        </p>

        <p>
          There is a second, less obvious reason. Sealant needs depth to flex. The rule of thumb is a bead roughly half as deep as it is wide, so a quarter-inch joint wants an eighth of an inch of material. Old caulk left in the bottom of the joint steals that depth. What you end up with is a shallow smear that has nowhere to stretch, and it splits down the middle at the first cold snap.
        </p>

        <p>
          The third reason is simply cosmetic, and it is the one most people notice first. New sealant applied over old lumps follows their contours. Every ridge, every crumb, every place where the previous person's finger stuttered gets faithfully reproduced in the new bead. You cannot tool a flat line over an uneven base.
        </p>

        <h2 id="identify" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Identify What You're Removing
        </h2>

        <p>
          The approach changes considerably depending on what is in the joint, and the identification takes about ten seconds. Press a fingernail into the bead. If it dents and springs back with a rubbery feel, it is silicone or a silicone hybrid. If it dents and stays dented, or feels chalky and slightly brittle, it is acrylic latex. If it is rock hard and barely marks, it is likely a cured polyurethane or a construction adhesive somebody used in place of sealant.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Acrylic latex</strong> is the easiest. It is water-based, it does not bond aggressively, and it frequently comes away in long strips with nothing more than a utility knife and a pull. Older acrylic that has gone chalky can crumble instead, which is messier but not harder.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Silicone</strong> is the difficult one. It bonds tenaciously to glazed and non-porous surfaces, it does not soften with heat in any useful way, and it leaves an invisible film behind that repels almost every other sealant. Silicone is also the material most likely to be in a bathroom, which is where most caulk removal happens.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Polyurethane and hybrid polymers</strong> sit between the two. They cut cleanly with a sharp blade and their bond to a porous substrate such as wood or masonry can be genuinely strong. Expect to leave a thin skin behind and to need mechanical abrasion to get it off.
        </p>

        <p>
          One more distinction worth making before you start: is this a joint that should be sealed at all? Plenty of caulk exists in places it was never meant to go — over weep holes, along the bottom edge of a window sill, across the gap where a wall meets a floor that needs to drain. If you are removing a bead from one of those places, the correct next step may be to leave it open rather than to replace it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. Tools That Actually Work
        </h2>

        <p>
          The toolkit for this job is short and cheap, and the temptation to substitute is what causes most of the collateral damage.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>A sharp utility knife with fresh blades.</strong> The single most important item. A dull blade tears the bead instead of scoring it and skates into the substrate. Change the blade far more often than feels reasonable — every two or three windows, or once per tub wall.</li>
          <li><strong>A dedicated caulk removal tool.</strong> The plastic ones with a hooked profile cost a few dollars and are genuinely good at lifting a scored bead out of an inside corner without gouging the tile.</li>
          <li><strong>A plastic razor scraper.</strong> For shaving residue off glazed tile, acrylic tubs and vinyl frames without scratching. Keep a metal razor for glass and glazed porcelain only.</li>
          <li><strong>A silicone digester or caulk softener.</strong> Applied over the bead and left for two to eight hours, these swell cured silicone so it releases its grip. They do not dissolve it — they make scraping possible.</li>
          <li><strong>Mineral spirits or isopropyl alcohol.</strong> For the final wipe. Alcohol is the safer default on plastics and painted surfaces; mineral spirits cut polyurethane residue more effectively.</li>
          <li><strong>An oscillating multi-tool with a flexible scraper blade.</strong> Not necessary, but on a long run of exterior polyurethane it turns an afternoon into an hour.</li>
          <li><strong>Abrasive pads.</strong> A non-scratch nylon pad for tubs and tile, and something coarser for masonry or bare wood.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
          What to avoid: screwdrivers, chisels, wire brushes on anything glazed, and heat guns near vinyl or acrylic. Every one of those leaves marks that are far more permanent than the caulk was.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-method" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. The Method, Step by Step
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Step one — soften if you need to.</strong> For silicone, or for any bead you have already tried to lift without success, lay a softener over the joint and leave it for the full dwell time on the label. Skipping this to save two hours routinely costs three hours of scraping.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Step two — score the first edge.</strong> Run the utility knife along the line where the bead meets the first surface, blade angled so it follows the substrate rather than digging into it. You are cutting the adhesion line, not slicing the bead in half. One continuous pass beats a dozen short stabs.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Step three — score the second edge.</strong> Repeat on the other face. The bead is now free on both sides and attached only by whatever grip remains at the back of the joint.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Step four — lift and pull.</strong> Work the point of the removal tool or the blade under one end and lift until you have an inch of free material. Grip it and pull along the joint at a shallow angle, steadily. Done well, an entire tub wall comes away as one continuous rubber worm, which is the most satisfying moment in home maintenance.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Step five — clear the joint.</strong> Go back along the run with the hooked tool and dig out anything left in the corner. In an inside corner there is almost always a triangle of material at the very back that the pull did not take. It has to come out.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Step six — shave the film.</strong> Hold a plastic razor almost flat to the surface and push along both faces. On silicone you will see a faint haze lift as a fine dust. This is the step people skip and the step that decides whether the new bead sticks.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Step seven — abrade and wipe.</strong> Give both faces a pass with an abrasive pad, then wipe with alcohol or mineral spirits on a clean rag, turning the rag frequently so you are not redistributing what you just lifted. Let it flash off completely.
        </p>

        <h2 id="surfaces" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Surface-by-Surface Notes
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bathtubs and Showers</h3>

        <p>
          Fill an acrylic or fibreglass tub with water before you recaulk — not before you remove. The weight deflects the tub downward to roughly its in-use position, so the new bead is installed at the joint's widest rather than being stretched every time somebody steps in. Remove the old caulk with the tub empty, because you want dry, accessible surfaces, then fill it before the new bead goes on.
        </p>

        <p>
          Watch out for mould that has grown behind the bead into the substrate. Once the caulk is out, if the grout line or the tub edge still shows black staining, no amount of new sealant will hide it — treat the area, let it dry thoroughly, and only then proceed. Sealing over damp, colonised grout guarantees the stain reappears through the new bead within months.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Windows and Exterior Trim</h3>

        <p>
          Exterior beads are usually longer runs of tougher material, often polyurethane, and frequently painted over. Cut through the paint film first with the knife so you are not tearing a strip of finish off the siding when you pull. On vinyl and fibre cement, keep the blade angle shallow — both mark easily and the marks are visible for the life of the wall.
        </p>

        <p>
          If backer rod was used, it will usually come out with the bead. Replace it rather than reusing it; the old rod has taken a compression set and will no longer size correctly.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Painted Interior Trim</h3>

        <p>
          Interior trim caulk is almost always acrylic and almost always painted. Score along the paint line, expect to pull some paint with it, and accept that you will be touching up afterwards. Trying to remove trim caulk without disturbing the paint is a losing battle — plan for a repaint of that edge and the job goes much faster.
        </p>

        <h2 id="final-prep" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Getting the Joint Truly Clean
        </h2>

        <p>
          There is a simple test for whether a joint is ready. Run a clean fingertip along both faces. It should feel like the material it is made of — the slick of glaze, the tooth of paint, the grain of wood. If it feels waxy, slightly slippery, or noticeably smoother than the surface an inch away, there is still a residue film and the new sealant will not bond to it.
        </p>

        <p>
          The second test is water. Mist the joint faces lightly. Water should wet out evenly across the surface. If it beads up and sits in droplets, that is silicone residue advertising itself, and the fix is more abrasion and another solvent wipe rather than more optimism.
        </p>

        <p>
          Finally, let the joint dry properly. Solvent wipes flash off in minutes but water rinses do not, and a porous substrate such as unsealed grout, masonry or bare timber can hold moisture for hours. Sealant applied to a damp porous surface bonds to a layer of wet dust rather than to the material. On a bathroom job, an overnight wait between removal and recaulking costs nothing and removes the single most common cause of premature failure.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Caulking straight over the old bead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The new sealant is only as well attached as the layer beneath it, and it has nowhere near enough depth to flex. It looks fine for a season and then lifts in one piece.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Using a dull blade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A blunt knife will not cut the adhesion line, so it skids off the bead and into the tub, the tile or the window frame. Blades are cents each; a gouged acrylic tub is not.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Skipping the residue shave</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Once the visible bead is gone the job looks finished. It is not — the invisible silicone film left behind is precisely what stops the next bead sticking.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Recaulking onto a damp joint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Grout, timber and masonry hold water far longer than the surface suggests. Sealant applied to a damp porous face bonds to moisture rather than substrate.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Reaching for a screwdriver</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Every scratch, chip and dent from an improvised tool outlasts the caulk you were trying to remove. A five-dollar removal tool prevents all of them.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the easiest way to remove old caulk?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Score both edges of the bead with a sharp utility knife or a caulk removal tool, then pull the freed strip out in one length. Most of the work is in the scoring — once both adhesion lines are cut, even old silicone usually lifts in long ribbons rather than crumbs.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Does vinegar remove caulk?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Not meaningfully. Vinegar softens some soap scum and mineral deposits on top of a bead, which makes it look like progress, but it does not break down cured silicone or polyurethane. Mechanical removal plus a dedicated softener is the only reliable route.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you caulk over old caulk?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Only if the existing bead is sound, fully bonded, clean and chemically compatible — and never over silicone, which almost nothing bonds to. Even in the best case you are relying on the old bead's adhesion rather than the substrate's, so a full removal is always the stronger result.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you remove silicone caulk residue?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">After the bulk is out, work a plastic scraper or a razor held almost flat along the surface to shave off the thin film, then wipe with mineral spirits or isopropyl alcohol. Silicone residue is invisible but it will stop new sealant bonding, so this step is not optional.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What dissolves caulk?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Purpose-made silicone digesters swell and soften cured silicone over two to eight hours so it can be scraped away. Mineral spirits soften some polyurethanes. Nothing genuinely dissolves cured sealant into liquid — softeners just reduce the mechanical effort required.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to remove caulk from a bathtub?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">A standard three-wall alcove tub takes roughly 45 to 90 minutes if the caulk is acrylic, and two to three hours for old silicone that needs a softener dwell. Rushing the cleanup is the usual reason a fresh bathroom bead fails within a year.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a heat gun help remove caulk?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">On acrylic latex, gentle heat softens the bead usefully. On silicone it does very little, and on acrylic tubs, vinyl windows or painted trim it risks scorching or warping the substrate. Use it sparingly, at low heat, and never on plastics.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to remove all the old caulk before recaulking?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes. Any material left in the joint is a weak layer between the substrate and the new bead, and it also occupies depth the new sealant needs in order to flex. Fragments left in corners are the most common cause of a bead that splits within a season.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Related Caulking Guides
        </h2>

        <ul>
          <li><a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">Best Exterior Caulk</a> — what to put back once the joint is clean.</li>
          <li><a href="/how-to-caulk-exterior-windows/">How to Caulk Exterior Windows</a> — the application side of the same job.</li>
          <li><a href="/silicone-vs-latex-caulk/">Silicone vs Latex Caulk</a> — which one you are pulling out, and which should replace it.</li>
          <li><a href="/caulk-vs-sealant/">Caulk vs Sealant</a> — why the distinction matters more than the label suggests.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 rounded-2xl p-8 text-gray-200">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            Removing old caulk is a patience job, not a strength job. Sharp blades, two clean scoring passes, a steady pull, and then the unglamorous work of shaving residue and getting the joint genuinely dry. That last half hour is what buys you the next fifteen years.
          </p>
          <p class="m-0">
            If you take one thing from this: the new bead cannot be better than the surface it lands on. Every premature caulk failure we have looked at traces back to something left in the joint, not to the product in the tube.
          </p>
